Subject: fs/system: both data roots resolve to Documents on iOS, cache to Caches

Upstream picks $E from the executable's directory and $H from
SDL_GetPrefPath(). On iOS the first is a read-only bundle and the second is
~/Library/Application Support/perfectdark - real, writable and invisible in the
Files app, which is where the player has to be able to drop their ROM.

So on iOS:
  - $E (exe dir), $H (home) and $S (save dir) all become the app's Documents
    directory. pd.ini, eeprom.bin, crashreports/, ghosts/, screenshots/,
    texture-packs/, model-packs/, xbla/ and mods/ therefore all land in one
    Files-visible folder, which is also the onboarding surface.
  - The base dir defaults to Documents itself rather than Documents/data, so
    pd.ntsc-final.z64 dropped straight into the app's folder is found. A
    Documents/data/ folder is still honoured if one exists.
  - The savedir legacy/migration pick is skipped: there is no older location on
    a platform this port has never shipped on, and $H is Documents anyway.
  - A new $C placeholder expands to Caches, and fsChooseOutputDir() routes the
    one directory that must not be in Documents - cache/, which is 250MB of
    regenerable XBLA unpack - there. Caches is not backed up and the OS may
    purge it; the importer already re-unpacks when its marker is gone.
  - sysLogSetPath() writes pd.log to Documents. An iOS app's working directory
    is "/" and is not writable, so upstream's "./pd.log first" would silently
    produce no log at all.

The two paths come from the environment (PD_IOS_DOCUMENTS / PD_IOS_CACHES),
which the shell sets before it calls the engine. That keeps the engine free of
Foundation and keeps this plain C.

